e": "Philippe de Levis", "context": "Pope Sixtus IV elevated Philippe de Lévis to the cardinalate in the consistory of May 7, 1473."}, {"sourceSlug": "philippe-de-levis", "sourceName": "Philippe de Levis", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "catholic-church-hierarchy", "targetName": "Catholic Church Hierarchy", "context": "Philippe de Lévis's career as a cardinal reinforced the trend of aristocratic influence and appointments within the Catholic Church hierarchy in the 15th century."}], "places": [{"name": "Arles, France", "role": "Archbishopric"}, {"name": "Rome, Papal States", "role": "Cardinalate location"}, {"name": "Agde, France", "role": "First bishopric"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Philippe de Levis", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "philippe-de-levis"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q2087776", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philippe_de_L%C3%A9vis"], "tags": ["France", "Religious Figures", "Europe", "comparative-religion", "doctrine-development", "cultural-diffusion"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 6, "significanceNarrative": "Philippe de Lévis's career as Archbishop of Arles and Cardinal exemplifies the confluence of aristocratic power and ecclesiastical authority in 15th-century France, influencing church-state relations and the composition of the papal curia. His rise reflects broader patterns of French royal influence within the Church and the strategic importance of noble clerics.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-14T06:53:12Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#29312614555", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Philippe de Lévis (1435–1475) was a French Roman Catholic bishop and cardinal.", "newValue": "Philippe de Lévis (1435–1475), a scion of the influential Lévis noble family, rose to prominence as a French Roman Catholic bishop and cardinal during the late Medieval period. His ecclesiastical career, spanning from his appointment as Bishop of Agde in 1461 to his elevation to the cardinalate in 1473, placed him at the nexus of French royal power and papal authority. De Lévis's trajectory exemplifies the intricate relationship between aristocratic lineage, political maneuvering, and religious office within the Kingdom of France, particularly under King Louis XI and during the pontificate of Pope Sixtus IV.

His most significant ecclesiastical appointment came in 1462, when he was named Archbishop of Arles, a prestigious see with a rich historical legacy in Provence. As Archbishop, de Lévis exercised considerable temporal and spiritual jurisdiction, navigating the complex political landscape of southern France, which was then undergoing consolidation under the French crown. His elevation to Cardinal-Deacon of Santi Marcellino e Pietro by Pope Sixtus IV on May 7, 1473, solidified his influence within the College of Cardinals and the broader European Church, granting him a voice in papal elections and significant diplomatic leverage.

